WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.120
As the Coronavirus pandemic caused
panicked shoppers to clear out whole supermarkets

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:11.820
of toilet paper, rice, and canned vegetables, two brothers set out on a 1300 mile road trip

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.300
through the backroads of Kentucky and Tennessee.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:21.740
Matt and Noah Colvin loaded up a U-haul with all the hand sanitizer and antibacterial wipes they could find,

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:26.280
and they attempted to resell the goods on Amazon at a massive markup.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:32.440
Then the online retailing giant banned the practice. State attorneys generals started cracking down

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:37.420
and the Colvin's were shamed in the media. They ended up donating the items.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:41.560
But is so-called price gouging always bad?

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:46.660
Michael Munger who teaches economics, political science, and public policy, at Duke University

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:51.760
argues that prices contain vital information about supply and demand.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:55.480
When government's attempt to mandate cheap goods, he says,

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:01.740
they end up causing more shortages than they solve. And this is especially true during a crisis.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:07.640
I spoke with Munger via Zoom about how the 21st century is testing libertarian ideas

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:11.000
about limited government and individual freedom.

